  "text": "Certainly! Here's a Python function that meets your requirements. It:\n\n- Converts the input string to lowercase.\n- Removes punctuation.\n- Splits the string into words.\n- Builds a dictionary where each key is a word and the value is its count.\n\n### \u2705 Solution Code:\n\n```python\nimport string\n\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    # Convert to lowercase\n    text = text.lower()\n    \n    # Remove punctuation using string.punctuation\n    for char in string.punctuation:\n        text = text.replace(char, '')\n    \n    # Split the text into words\n    words = text.split()\n    \n    # Create the histogram dictionary\n    histogram = {}\n    for word in words:\n        histogram[word] = histogram.get(word, 0) + 1\n    \n    return histogram\n```\n\n### \ud83d\udd0d Explanation:\n\n- `text.lower()` makes everything lowercase to ensure case-insensitivity.\n- `string.punctuation` contains all punctuation characters (`!\"#$%&'()*+,-./:;<=>?@[$$^_`{|}~`), and we loop through them to remove any occurrence from the string.\n- `text.split()` splits the cleaned text into individual words.\n- We then count the frequency of each word using a dictionary and the `get()` method for safe access.\n\n### \u2705 Example:\n\n```python\nprint(word_histogram(\"Hello, world! Hello.\"))  # Output: {'hello': 2, 'world': 1}\nprint(word_histogram(\"Python is great. Python is fun!\"))  # Output: {'python': 2, 'is': 2, 'great': 1, 'fun': 1}\nprint(word_histogram(\"Test, test, TEST.\"))  # Output: {'test': 3}\n```\n\nLet me know if you\u2019d like this to handle more complex punctuation or edge cases!",
